WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:09.260
Recently, a new breed of AI techniques surfaced
that were capable of this new thing called

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.840
image translation.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:16.230
And by image translation I mean that they
can translate a drawn map to a satellite image,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:22.699
take a set of color labels and make a photorealistic
facade, or take a sketch and create a photo

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:23.699
out of it.

00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:26.679
This is done through a Generative Adversarial
Network.

00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:31.099
This is an architecture where we have a pair
of neural networks, one that learns to generate

00:00:31.099 --> 00:00:36.000
new images, and the other learns to tell a
fake image from a real one.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.920
As they compete against each other, they get
better and better without any human interaction.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.370
In these earlier applications, unfortunately,
the output is typically one image, and since

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:51.240
there are many possible shoes that could satisfy
our initial sketch, it is highly unlikely

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.010
that the one we are offered is exactly what
we envisioned.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:59.910
This improved version enhanced this algorithm
to be able to produce not one, but an entire

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.579
set of outputs.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:06.240
And as you can see here, we have a night image,
and a set of potential daytime translations

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.330
on the right that are quite diverse.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.460
I really like how it has an intuitive understanding
of the illumination differences of the building

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.130
during night and daytime.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.719
It really seems to know how to add lighting
to the building.

00:01:18.719 --> 00:01:23.619
It also models the atmospheric scattering
during daytime, creates multiple kinds of

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:27.700
pretty convincing clouds, or puts hills in
the background.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.450
The results are both realistic, and the additional
selling point is that this technique offers

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.850
an entire selection of outputs.

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:39.210
What I found to be really cool about the next
comparisons is that ground truth images are

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.229
also attached for reference.

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:46.179
If we can take a photograph of a city at nighttime,
we have access to the same view during the

00:01:46.179 --> 00:01:51.611
daytime too, or we can take a photograph of
a shoe and draw the outline of it by hand.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:55.990
And as you can see here, there are not only
lots of high-quality outputs, but in some

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.710
cases, the ground truth image is really well
approximated by the algorithm.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:04.709
This means that we give it a crude drawing,
and it could translate this drawing into a

00:02:04.709 --> 00:02:06.639
photorealistic image that is very close to
reality.

00:02:06.639 --> 00:02:08.570
I think that is mind blowing!

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:13.100
The validation section of the paper reveals
that this technique provides a great tradeoff

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.480
between diversity and quality.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:21.020
There are previous methods that perform well
if we need one high-quality solution, or many

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:26.660
not so great ones, but overall this one provides
a great package for artists working in the

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:31.090
industry, and this will be a godsend for any
kind of content generation scenario.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.570
The source code of this project is also available,
and make sure to read the license before starting

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:36.570
your experiments.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

